<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Dian</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dianchi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Dian, or Dianchi, is a large freshwater lake on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guizhou">Guizhou</a> Plateau beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunming">Kunming</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, the largest lake in Yunnan. Its shores were the centre of the Dian kingdom, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> culture of the last centuries BCE known for its bronze drums and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cowrie-shell">cowrie</a>-shell containers decorated with figures. Its royal cemeteries at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shizhaishan">Shizhaishan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lijiashan">Lijiashan</a> lie near the lake.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-dian.jpg" alt="Lake Dian" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: glxcn · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%AD%A3%E5%A4%A7%E6%B2%B3%E4%B8%8E%E9%87%91%E5%AE%B6%E6%B2%B3%E4%BA%A4%E6%B1%87%E5%85%A5%E6%BB%87%E6%B1%A0%E5%8F%A3%E8%BF%9C%E7%9C%BA%E8%A5%BF%E5%B1%B1_-_panoramio_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dian Lake lies on the Puduhe-Xishan fault in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunming">Kunming</a> and is a shallow freshwater fault lake. It runs about 40 km from north to south, averages 7 km in width, reaches 12.5 km at its widest and has some 150 km of coastline. It stands 1,886 m above sea level and covers 298 km2, with an average depth of 4.4 m. An artificial dike divides it into Caohai in the north and the much larger Waihai in the south. Its water leaves to the west by the Pudu River, which joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>. The Kunming Lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>'s Summer Palace was modelled on it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Chinese character for the lake, 滇, is first found in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a> as the name of the Dian Kingdom. Modern studies suggest it comes from the language of the extinct Bo people and means basin. The character has named the lake since at least 109 BCE, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> established Dianchi County. In the first millennium CE the lake was the site of the capital of the independent kingdom of the Cuan, then known as Kunchuan. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a> the Shu Han forces led by their chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuge-liang">Zhuge Liang</a> visited it in triumph after putting down a rebellion of the Nanman tribes.</p><p>Between 1958 and 1970 about 54 square kilometres of marsh were reclaimed for industry and agriculture. A rally held on 28 December 1969 mobilised 100,000 soldiers with students and factory workers, and the campaign was led by Lieutenant General Tan Furen, who died by assassination in 1970.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Pollution became the lake's major problem. Until the first wastewater plant was built in 1990, ninety percent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunming">Kunming</a>'s wastewater was dumped untreated into it. By the late 1990s it was named among the three leading water pollution concerns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and in 1995 it was listed in the Three Rivers and Three Lakes sewage-treatment scheme. Wetland parks have since been built along its shore. Of 25 native fish species and subspecies, 10 were endemic to the basin, and Anabarilius alburnops is the only one known to survive in the lake itself. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> lake newt has not been seen since 1979 and is considered extinct.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dian Lake” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dian grew among the farming and fishing peoples around <b>Lake Dian</b>, near Kunming.</blockquote><a href="/state/dian-kingdom">Dian Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
